<statement>
<p>
	This function will display the guess boxes on the game board by pulling information that is stored in the &lt;pf&gt;guess_boxes&lt;/pf&gt; struct.
</p>

<tabular valign="top" top="minor">
	<col width="12%"/>
	<col width="88%"/>
	<row bottom="minor">
		<cell>Inputs:</cell>
		<cell>
			<p><pf>guess_boxes</pf> (6 5 struct with 4 fields) number — value</p>
		</cell>
	</row>
	<row bottom="minor">
		<cell>Outputs:</cell>
		<cell>
			<p><pf>no</pf> number — function outputs, but the guess box portion of the game board is displayed</p>
		</cell>
	</row>
	<row>
		<cell>Details:</cell>
		<cell><p>Download the template provided to get started.</p></cell>
	</row>
	<row>
		<cell/>
		<cell><p>In order to test this function, you should save</p></cell>
	</row>
	<row>
		<cell/>
		<cell><p>this .mat file</p></cell>
	</row>
	<row bottom="minor">
		<cell/>
		<cell><p>that contains some sample input in the space where you are running code. Use the load command as shown and you will see the four sample inputs in the workspace. Then you can test your function, as shown</p></cell>
	</row>
</tabular>

<p>
	Examples:
<program>
	<code>
		INPUT
		guess_boxes1
		OUTPUT
		INPUT
		guess_boxes2
		OUTPUT
		INPUT
		guess_boxes3
		OUTPUT
		INPUT
		guess_boxes4
		OUTPUT
	</code>
</program>
</p>
</statement>
